course: dictionary
lesson: 11227
title: Wool: one word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. «Wool» can mean the soft, thick hair that grows on sheep, goats and alpacas. It can also mean the yarn or cloth made from that hair. The words around it tell you which meaning the author wants.
explain: «They gave milk, wool, meat, eggs and muscle power» is about what animals give to people, so wool means the hair itself. Look for clues like knitting, jumpers or scarves, and you know the cloth or yarn is meant.
explain: Steel wool is a third meaning, and the word «steel» shows it. It is a pad of thin metal threads for scrubbing, not something a sheep grows. Always check the words next to the hard word before you decide what it means.
fact: Wool is the soft, thick hair of sheep, goats and alpacas.
fact: Wool can also mean the yarn or cloth made from this hair, used for jumpers, hats and scarves.
fact: Steel wool is a pad of thin metal threads used for scrubbing.
fact: Wool keeps you warm because it is an insulator: heat moves through it slowly, and it traps air.
fact: «Farm sheep grow wool all the time and must be shorn every year.» A sheep called Shrek in New Zealand escaped shearing for six years and grew 27 kilograms of wool.
exercise: «Farm sheep grow wool all the time and must be shorn every year.» What is wool in this sentence? | A metal pad for scrubbing | A kind of warm sweet | *The soft hair that grows on a sheep | A wooden fence post
exercise: Mia knits a warm scarf with a ball of wool. What is the wool here? | *Yarn spun from hair, ready for knitting | The sheep that gave the hair | A jumper already finished | Steel threads for scrubbing
exercise: Which of these is steel wool? | Alpaca hair for a hat | A soft woolly jumper | *A pad of thin metal threads for scrubbing | Wool on a sheep's back
test: Which animals give wool? | Cows and pigs | *Sheep, goats and alpacas | Hens and ducks | Horses and camels
test: Why does a wool jumper keep you warm? | Wool is a conductor, so heat rushes out quickly | Wool is wet, so it cools you down | *Wool is an insulator, so heat moves through it slowly | Wool is made of metal, so it holds heat
exam: Tom says, «He tried to pull the wool over his teacher's eyes.» What does this phrase mean? | To shear a sheep quickly | *To trick someone | To knit someone a hat | To cover someone with a warm blanket
